  "schema_version": "bookstory.v1",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Building Collaborative Governance in Times of Uncertainty has attracted measurable scholarly attention, with 12 citations in OpenAlex and a field-normalized standing in the top 1% for its year and discipline. The book also shows active use through 2,017 OAPEN downloads, alongside explicit alignment with SDG 17, Partnerships for the Goals, and funding support from the Diputaci\u00f3n Foral de Gipuzkoa.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This book has established clear early scholarly uptake and unusually strong relative citation performance for a 2023 publication. Its citation record is still modest in absolute terms, but the field-normalized percentile indicates that it is performing at the very top of its peer set. That pattern is consistent with a work that is being taken up quickly and selectively within research on public administration, collaborative governance, and democratic accountability.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape suggests that the book is resonating most strongly in public policy and administration research, while also reaching adjacent conversations in sustainability governance, management studies, gender and representation, and organizational analysis. The venues that cite it include specialist journals in public administration and policy, which points to disciplinary relevance rather than broad general-audience diffusion. Several of the citing works are themselves concerned with collaborative governance, deliberation, trust, and public-sector partnerships, indicating that the book is already helping to shape follow-on conceptual and empirical work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence reinforces that this is not just a citation-based signal. More than two thousand downloads through OAPEN indicate substantial reading interest, especially for an open hybrid-access book. The record also shows a verified funding relationship with the Diputaci\u00f3n Foral de Gipuzkoa, which fits the book\u2019s case-based engagement with collaborative governance practice and its policy-facing orientation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The available record is strongest for scholarly and usage impact. It does not include event data or public-discourse archiving, so the story here is one of demonstrable academic uptake and active consultation rather than broad media visibility. Taken together, the evidence shows a book that is already circulating within relevant research communities, being read widely in open-access form, and connecting with policy-relevant agendas around partnerships, democracy, and collaborative governance under uncertainty.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
